== J.1 Prose

#block(
  width: 100%,
  fill: rgb("#F8FAFC"),
  stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-border,
  radius: 8pt,
  inset: (x: 12pt, y: 10pt),
)[
  #set par(leading: 0.62em, spacing: 0.72em)
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[PASSAGE]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 9.6pt)[
    The train was late, and the platform had filled with the particular patience of people who have already given up being angry. A woman in a green wrapper sold oranges from a basin balanced on her head, and the queue for her basin was longer than the queue for the ticket window.

    Emeka stood at the far end, where the platform ran out of roof. He had bought a ticket for a town he had never seen, on the advice of a man he had met once, and he had told nobody at home. The rain began at the far end of the track — he could see it coming, a grey wall moving up the rails — and the platform crowd stirred and gathered its bags. Emeka did not move. He had decided, somewhere between the ticket window and the end of the roof, that getting wet was the first thing he was going to do on his own.
  ]
]

#mcq(1, [The detail about the orange seller chiefly], [shows that the station is busy], [measures the crowd's patience by what it will queue for], [introduces the protagonist], [indicates the time of year])

#mcq(2, [The rain is described as *a grey wall moving up the rails* in order to], [give the weather report], [make the approaching change visible before it arrives], [show that the train is late], [introduce a new character])

#mcq(3, [Emeka does not move because], [he has not seen the rain], [he has decided to accept the rain as his first independent act], [he is waiting for the train], [he has nowhere to go])

#mcq(4, [The narrative point of view is], [first person], [third person limited to Emeka], [third person omniscient], [third person objective])

#mcq(5, [The passage's central concern is], [railway travel], [the beginning of an independent life], [the price of oranges], [the weather in the rainy season])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "B", "B", "B", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [The queue for the basin is compared with the queue for tickets, so the detail measures the crowd's priorities. It also gives the platform a life of its own, which the passage needs before Emeka separates himself from it.]),
  ("B", [A wall moving up the rails is an image of approach, and it makes the coming change visible while Emeka is still deciding. Weather reporting would not need the movement.]),
  ("B", [The last sentence states the decision: getting wet is the first thing he does on his own. His stillness is therefore a choice, not an oversight.]),
  ("B", [His thoughts and memories are reported and no other mind is entered, which is third person limited. Objective narration would give no access to his decision.]),
  ("B", [The ticket, the secrecy, the refusal to move: the passage is about a first independent act. Travel is the occasion, not the subject.]),
))

== J.2 Drama

#block(
  width: 100%,
  fill: rgb("#F8FAFC"),
  stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-border,
  radius: 8pt,
  inset: (x: 12pt, y: 10pt),
)[
  #set par(leading: 0.58em, spacing: 0.8em)
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[EXTRACT]
  #v(4pt)
  #set text(size: 9.8pt)
  (A room above a shop. BOLA and her brother TUNDE. A suitcase, open, on the floor.) \
  TUNDE: You have not told her. \
  BOLA: I am telling her tonight. \
  TUNDE: You said that on Tuesday. \
  BOLA: (folding a dress) On Tuesday the shop was open. \
  TUNDE: The shop will always be open. That is what a shop does. \
  BOLA: And I will always be here. That is what I do. \
  TUNDE: (quietly) She will not stop you. \
  BOLA: I know. \
  TUNDE: Then why are you whispering? \
  (BOLA puts the dress in the case and closes it. The latch is loud.) \
]

#mcq(6, [The open suitcase on stage chiefly functions to], [show that the room is untidy], [make the departure visible before it is spoken], [indicate a change of scene], [introduce Tunde])

#mcq(7, [Tunde's *You said that on Tuesday* is], [a factual correction], [an accusation that turns her promise into a habit of delay], [a joke], [a stage direction])

#mcq(8, [The exchange about the shop is built on], [a long speech and a reply], [parallel constructions that set her life against the shop's], [comic relief], [a soliloquy])

#mcq(9, [The loud latch is], [a sound effect only], [a stage action that makes the decision final], [a sign that the case is broken], [an exit cue])

#mcq(10, [Bola's whispering shows that], [she has a cold], [she is already behaving as though she has left], [she is afraid of Tunde], [the room is large])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "B", "B", "B", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [The suitcase is on stage before any line is spoken, so the audience reads the departure before it is discussed. That is exposition done by a prop.]),
  ("B", [The repetition of the promise, with the earlier date, converts the promise into evidence. It accuses without raising its voice, which is how the scene keeps its pressure low and its tension high.]),
  ("B", [*The shop will always be open* answers *I will always be here*: two balanced constructions, each defining a life. The symmetry is the argument.]),
  ("B", [The latch closes the case and the conversation at once. A stage sound that ends a scene is an action, and it is the decision made audible.]),
  ("B", [She whispers about a departure she has not announced, which is the behaviour of someone already gone. The line is the scene's best piece of psychology, and it is delivered as a question by her brother.]),
))

== J.3 Poetry

#block(
  width: 100%,
  fill: rgb("#F8FAFC"),
  stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-border,
  radius: 8pt,
  inset: (x: 14pt, y: 10pt),
)[
  #set par(leading: 0.58em, spacing: 0.8em)
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[POEM]
  #v(4pt)
  #set text(size: 9.9pt)
  The market does not close. \
  It puts its lights out one stall at a time \
  and keeps the conversation. \
  \
  My mother's stall was third from the gate. \
  She sold cloth, and she could tell \
  a bad thread by touch, in the dark, \
  which is a skill nobody pays for. \
  \
  They have built a bank where the gate was. \
  The bank has a clock. \
  The clock is always right, and nobody \
  stands under it to talk. \
]

#mcq(11, [The poem's first stanza suggests that the market], [is closing down for ever], [has a life that continues after its lights go out], [is badly lit], [is empty at night])

#mcq(12, [The detail that the mother could tell a bad thread *in the dark* chiefly], [shows that she was poor], [makes her skill bodily and unrewarded], [proves she was old], [criticises the market])

#mcq(13, [The bank and the clock stand for], [progress and accuracy], [a world of measurement that has replaced a world of conversation], [the poet's childhood], [the government])

#mcq(14, [The poem is written in], [iambic pentameter], [free verse with short lines], [ballad stanza], [rhyming couplets])

#mcq(15, [The tone of the last stanza is best described as], [angry], [elegiac and quietly reproachful], [comic], [indifferent])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "B", "B", "B", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [Not closing but keeping the conversation: the stanza separates the official life of the market from its actual one, which is the poem's argument in miniature.]),
  ("B", [Skill in the dark is knowledge held in the hands, and the parenthesis — nobody pays for it — supplies the poem's judgment without a raised voice.]),
  ("B", [The clock is always right and nobody stands under it: accuracy has replaced company. The bank is an institution, and the poem's target is what institutions measure.]),
  ("B", [No regular metre and no rhyme, with line breaks doing the work — including the isolated *and keeps the conversation*. The poem is free verse.]),
  ("B", [The loss is stated without denunciation, and the flat final clause — nobody stands under it to talk — is more damning for being quiet.]),
))

#v(4pt)

#concept-box("The one habit these fifteen questions teach")[
  In every case the answer names a *consequence*: the detail measures something,
  the image makes something visible, the symmetry argues something. The wrong options
  are almost all descriptions — of the weather, the set, the shop, the clock. If an
  option describes and does not argue, it is a distractor.
]

#v(4pt)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[J.4 The three-question routine, in the hall]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[
    One: what genre, and therefore what may I quote? Two: what changes between the first
    line and the last? Three: which single device carries that change? If you can answer
    those three in the margin in ninety seconds, the five questions that follow are
    reading questions rather than guesswork.
  ]
]

